.SH A) OVERVIEW
all steps can be marked as conditionally run based on string of characters.
BUT, the filtering is only be called by REUSE and is only active in SHARED,
GLOBAL, and CONFIG; everywhere else, it will be ignored.  this ability creates
some very powerful opportunities to consolidate data and review.

.SH B) TECHNICAL
using filters creates a decision around each individual condition-level and
step-level verb -- run or skip.

.SH C) REQUESTS
REUSE filtering requests are based on a single, lower-case letter [a-z].
the example below will call the SHARED code identified as "a" with a filtering
request for the identifier "c".

   verb-------- § ---desc-------------- §
   ···REUSE a/c § call initial setep    §    runs SHARED "a" with filter "c"
   ···REUSE M/a § call initial setep    §    runs GLOBAL "M" with filter "a"
   ···REUSE ò/f § call initial setep    §    runs CONFIG "ò" with filter "f"

to request everything run, just use a normal "REUSE a" without a criteria.

.SH D) FILTERING
selection filters are added to descriptions, usually prefixed and must be
surrounded by test-braces "å···æ".  in this example, the spacers (·) are
just spacers with no meaning.  if there is not selection filter, the condition
or step will always run.

as shown in KOIOS_SHARE (5), REUSE will only allow a filtering code explicitly
allowed in the definition of SHARED, GLOBAL, or CONFIG.  assume for the example
only a, b, and c are explicitly defined.

   verb-------- § ---desc-------------- §
   ···COND      § åabcæ condition       §    always runs
   ·····exec    § åa··æ run something   §    runs only for a
   ·····exec    § å·b·æ run same thing  §    runs only for b
   ·····get     § åabcæ check result    §    always runs
   ·····echo    § å·bcæ grab return     §    runs for either b or c

the usefulness in this example is that calling with "a" will run one version
and check the result.  whereas, calling with "b", will run a different version,
do the same check, and the grab the result.  finally, "c" will only check
and grab the result.

NOTE:  if the condition only had åa·cæ, nothing would run for "b" as the
entire condition would be skipped.

.SH E) OUTPUT
a first example shows a normal share request with out a fiter, and the second
shows the same request with a filter of "a".

  CONFIG (÷/*) ==-------   set up details for sample polymnia   ---------==
  CONFIG (÷/a) ==-------   set up details for sample polymnia   ---------==

if a specific filter is requested, they alter only slightly.  when the reuse
is over, the footer for a normal run is the first example, and a run
filtered for "a" is shown second.

  GIFNOC (÷)--[ cond=56    test=16946 ]---( * >>>-- cond=56    test=16946 ]
  GIFNOC (÷)--[ cond=26    test=14864 ]---( a >>>-- cond=56    test=16946 ]

the first cond/test are what actual ran, the second cond/test are all available.

for all the step filtering, since the filter string is in the description,
it is easy to follow inclusions if necesssary.  BUT, for exclusions, there
is no notice other than the headers.



